    "['eth-trunk [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'channel-group 1 mode active', 'parent_command': ['interface [parameter1]'], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nchannel-group 1 mode active\\n##\\n\\nExplanation: \\n- In Cisco, `eth-trunk 1` (Huawei's command to add an interface to an EtherChannel/LAG) is equivalent to `channel-group` in Cisco IOS.\\n- The `mode active` specifies LACP active mode (similar to Huawei's default behavior when adding interfaces to an Eth-Trunk).\\n- The number `1` corresponds to the port-channel/PAGP group number (equivalent to Eth-Trunk 1).\", 'trans_template': 'channel-group 1 mode active'}]]": 1,
    "['eth-trunk [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'channel-group 1 mode active', 'parent_command': ['interface [parameter1]'], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nchannel-group 1 mode active\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Cisco, `eth-trunk 1` (Huawei's command to add an interface to an Eth-Trunk/LACP bundle) is equivalent to `channel-group` with a mode (like `active` for LACP).\\n- `1` is the channel-group number (equivalent to the Eth-Trunk ID).\\n- `mode active` enables LACP in active mode (similar to Huawei's default Eth-Trunk behavior).\", 'trans_template': 'channel-group 1 mode active'}]]": 1,
    "['bfd [parameter1] bind link-bundle peer-ip [parameter2] interface [parameter3] source-ip [parameter4]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'bfd interval 10 min_rx 10 multiplier 3', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'bfd interval 10 min_rx 10 multiplier 3', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nbfd interval 10 min_rx 10 multiplier 3\\nbfd echo\\nbfd bind peer-ipv4 10.1.1.2 interface Eth-Trunk1 source-ip 10.1.1.1\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. The Cisco equivalent requires separate commands for BFD parameters and session setup\\n2. \"bfd interval\" sets the transmit/receive timers (10ms in this case)\\n3. \"bfd echo\" enables echo mode (similar to Huawei\\'s link-bundle approach)\\n4. \"bfd bind\" establishes the session with peer IP and source IP specification\\n5. Cisco doesn\\'t have the exact \"link-bundle\" concept, but echo mode over a bundle interface achieves similar functionality\\n6. The multiplier (3) is added as Cisco requires it (default value shown)\\n\\nNote: For complete equivalence, you would need to configure the Eth-Trunk interface appropriately on Cisco, which would typically be a Port-Channel interface.', 'trans_template': 'bfd interval 10 min_rx 10 multiplier 3'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'bfd echo', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'bfd echo', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nbfd interval 10 min_rx 10 multiplier 3\\nbfd echo\\nbfd bind peer-ipv4 10.1.1.2 interface Eth-Trunk1 source-ip 10.1.1.1\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. The Cisco equivalent requires separate commands for BFD parameters and session setup\\n2. \"bfd interval\" sets the transmit/receive timers (10ms in this case)\\n3. \"bfd echo\" enables echo mode (similar to Huawei\\'s link-bundle approach)\\n4. \"bfd bind\" establishes the session with peer IP and source IP specification\\n5. Cisco doesn\\'t have the exact \"link-bundle\" concept, but echo mode over a bundle interface achieves similar functionality\\n6. The multiplier (3) is added as Cisco requires it (default value shown)\\n\\nNote: For complete equivalence, you would need to configure the Eth-Trunk interface appropriately on Cisco, which would typically be a Port-Channel interface.', 'trans_template': 'bfd echo'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'bfd bind peer-ipv4 10.1.1.2 interface Eth-Trunk1 source-ip 10.1.1.1', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'bfd bind peer-ipv4 10.1.1.2 interface Eth-Trunk1 source-ip 10.1.1.1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nbfd interval 10 min_rx 10 multiplier 3\\nbfd echo\\nbfd bind peer-ipv4 10.1.1.2 interface Eth-Trunk1 source-ip 10.1.1.1\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. The Cisco equivalent requires separate commands for BFD parameters and session setup\\n2. \"bfd interval\" sets the transmit/receive timers (10ms in this case)\\n3. \"bfd echo\" enables echo mode (similar to Huawei\\'s link-bundle approach)\\n4. \"bfd bind\" establishes the session with peer IP and source IP specification\\n5. Cisco doesn\\'t have the exact \"link-bundle\" concept, but echo mode over a bundle interface achieves similar functionality\\n6. The multiplier (3) is added as Cisco requires it (default value shown)\\n\\nNote: For complete equivalence, you would need to configure the Eth-Trunk interface appropriately on Cisco, which would typically be a Port-Channel interface.', 'trans_template': 'bfd bind peer-ipv4 10.1.1.2 interface Eth-Trunk1 source-ip 10.1.1.1'}]]": 1,
    "['min-tx-interval [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'bfd interval 10 min_tx 10', 'parent_command': ['bfd interval 10 min_rx 10 multiplier 3'], 'root': 'bfd interval 10 min_rx 10 multiplier 3', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent command for `min-tx-interval 10`:\\n\\n##\\nbfd interval 10 min_tx 10\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco's BFD configuration, the interval command combines both transmit and receive intervals. The syntax is `bfd interval <milliseconds> min_rx <milliseconds> min_tx <milliseconds>`. Since the original Huawei configuration sets both tx and rx intervals to 10 ms, the Cisco equivalent maintains this symmetry.\", 'trans_template': 'bfd interval 10 min_tx 10'}]]": 1,
    "['min-rx-interval [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'bfd interval 10 min_rx 10', 'parent_command': ['bfd interval 10 min_rx 10 multiplier 3'], 'root': 'bfd interval 10 min_rx 10 multiplier 3', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nbfd interval 10 min_rx 10\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- The HUAWEI `min-rx-interval 10` command sets the minimum BFD receive interval to 10 milliseconds.\\n- The equivalent Cisco command is `bfd interval <min_tx> min_rx <min_rx>`, where both intervals are specified together.\\n- Since the configuration shows both tx and rx intervals as 10 ms in HUAWEI, I've set both values to 10 in the Cisco version.\\n- Note that Cisco requires both intervals to be specified in the same command, unlike HUAWEI where they can be set separately.\", 'trans_template': 'bfd interval 10 min_rx 10'}]]": 1,
